# User-controlled agent orchestration: no automatic chaining or conflict-risk parallelism

When orchestrating multiple agent tasks, default to explicit, conservative scheduling: (1) check for shared file edits before running tasks in parallel — a single shared file mandates sequential execution; (2) never automatically trigger downstream phases (reviews, deploys, follow-on tickets) after a task completes — gate every phase transition on explicit user action. The overarching rule: the user owns the workflow rhythm; the agent owns individual task execution.

## Procedures
- Before scheduling N tasks, enumerate the files each task is expected to edit.
- If any two tasks share at least one file, run them sequentially regardless of theoretical independence.
- After a task completes, stop and surface the result; do not automatically queue the next logical phase (e.g., review, merge, deploy).
- Wait for explicit user instruction before advancing to the next workflow stage.

## Conventions
- Sequential > parallel whenever file overlap exists or is uncertain.
- Phase transitions (execute → review, review → merge) are always user-initiated.

